<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelias</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Greek mythology, Pelias was the king of Iolkos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> who seized the throne from his half-brother Aeson. Warned by an oracle to beware a man wearing one sandal, he sent Aeson's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> to fetch the Golden Fleece. He was later killed by his own daughters, whom Medea tricked into believing they could restore his youth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelias.jpg" alt="Pelias" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelias_meets_Jason_MAN_Napoli_Inv111436.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek myth Pelias was the son of Tyro and the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>. Tyro was married to King Cretheus of Iolcus, with whom she had three sons, Aeson, Pherês and Amythaon, but she loved the river god Enipeus. Poseidon disguised himself as Enipeus and lay with her, and the twins Pelias and Neleus were born. Tyro exposed them on a mountain, where a herdsman raised them, or in another account a maid. As adults they found their mother and killed her stepmother Sidero for mistreating her, which earned Pelias Hera's undying hatred.</p><p>Pelias wished to rule all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. He banished Neleus and Pherês and locked Aeson in the dungeons of Iolcus, near the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volos">Volos</a>. There Aeson married and had several children, of whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> was the best known. Aeson sent Jason away for fear that Pelias would kill him as a possible heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Pelias ruled Iolcus and had many children, among them Acastus and the daughters known together as the Peliades. An oracle warned him to beware a man wearing one sandal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a>, raised by the centaur Chiron on Mount Pelion, came to Iolcus when Pelias was offering a sacrifice to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> by the sea, having lost one sandal in the flooded river Anaurus. Pelias asked what Jason would do if he met the man destined to be his downfall, and Jason answered that he would send him after the Golden Fleece.</p><p>Pelias sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> to Colchis for the fleece, which hung on an oak in a grove sacred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> and was guarded by a dragon. Jason had the shipwright Argus build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a>, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> he won the fleece with the help of Medea, daughter of King Aeëtes. In his absence Pelias, thinking the Argo had sunk, told Aeson and Promachus so, and they killed themselves by poison. It is unknown, but possible, that Pelias killed them himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a>'s return Pelias still refused to give up the throne. Medea showed his daughters how she could turn an old ram into a young one by cutting it up and boiling it, and a live young ram leapt from the pot. The Peliades cut their father into pieces and threw him in a pot, expecting him to emerge rejuvenated, and he did not survive. Jason, now an accessory to the crime, was not made king, and Pelias's son Acastus banished Jason and Medea to Corinth and reclaimed the kingdom. At the funeral games for Pelias, Atalanta defeated Peleus at wrestling, a match that became a popular subject in Greek art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pelias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek myth made Iolkos the city of <b>Pelias</b> and the starting point of Jason's voyage on the Argo.</blockquote><a href="/state/mycenaean-kingdom-of-iolkos">Mycenaean Kingdom of Iolkos</a></li></ul></section>
